drm/panfrost: coordinate DVFS with runtime PM

A firmware-owned core clock can only be changed while the GPU power domain
is up. Firmware programming hardware in a domain that is down aborts, and
on RK3576 that arrives as an SError and takes the machine with it:

  SError Interrupt on CPU0, code 0x00000000bf000000
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Asynchronous SError Interrupt
  pc : smc_send_message+0x14c/0x160
    panfrost_devfreq_target [panfrost]
    dev_pm_opp_set_rate / clk_set_rate / scmi_clk_set_rate
    smc_send_message

Suspending devfreq stops the governor, but the userspace governor's
set_freq reaches panfrost_devfreq_target() straight from sysfs, so a
process able to write that file could panic the machine. Serialise
transitions on a mutex against an explicit availability flag, and record a
request that arrives while the hardware is away so resume can apply it.
Runtime PM status cannot serve as that flag: the core reports SUSPENDING
before the domain is down and RESUMING before it is back, so a check built
on it would defer devfreq's own suspend transition and leave the GPU
powering down on the firmware PLL.

Park and mark-unavailable happen under one lock, as do restore, reset and
becoming available again, because a request landing midway would otherwise
be recorded as deferred and never replayed. A deferred request returns
success rather than an error: devfreq only advances its own bookkeeping on
success, so an error leaves it retrying every poll for as long as the
device stays idle. Requested and applied rates are tracked separately -
devfreq holds the requested policy, current_frequency records what the
hardware is running.

The parking rate is per-SoC driver data, hung off a rockchip,rk3576-mali
match that the DTS and binding already carry. It goes through
dev_pm_opp_set_rate() rather than clk_set_rate() even though 200 MHz is
below the lowest OPP: the OPP core rounds to the ceiling OPP for voltage
while programming the clock to exactly that rate, and records it in
current_rate_single_clk. A raw clk_set_rate() leaves that bookkeeping at
the pre-park OPP, so restoring the same OPP afterwards compares equal,
returns early, and the clock never comes back up.

While here, propagate the devfreq_suspend_device() failure that runtime
suspend was discarding. Its suspend_count is incremented before anything
that can fail and is never unwound, so the error path balances it rather
than leaving devfreq suspended for good.

Measured on an ArmSoM Sige5, reading scmi_clk_gpu from debugfs rather than
devfreq's cur_freq, which is only previous_freq here since panfrost
declares no get_cur_freq:

  requested   300  400  500  600  700  800  900 MHz
  actual      300  400  500  600  700  800  900 MHz

  300 -> suspend -> resume:  300, park 200, 300
  900 -> suspend -> resume:  900, park 200, 900

  100 forced suspend/resume cycles under a writer alternating 300 and 900:
  100/100 parked correctly, no SError, no warnings.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_devfreq.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panfrost/panfrost_devfreq.c
